#version 450

// Projective texturing, single-camera (live line-of-sight) slice. One thread per
// VERTEX: project it into the posed camera and give it a normalized atlas UV
// (into the camera image the caller binds as the atlas) when it is in front,
// inside the image, and unoccluded -- its projected depth agreeing with the
// sensor depth map within pc.occlusion_threshold.
//
// Per vertex rather than per triangle, and that is the whole shape of this
// kernel. The visibility verdict reads nothing but the vertex's own position,
// the camera and the depth map (project_to_image + occluded_ok below), so it
// was always a pure function of the vertex; deciding it per triangle and then
// writing the result to three vertices was what made this pass incompatible
// with MarchingCubesConfig::share_vertices, where one vertex belongs to up to
// six triangles that can disagree. Dispatching per vertex removes the shared
// write outright -- there is no second writer to race, no index buffer to read,
// and each vertex is projected once instead of once per referencing triangle
// (~3x less work on a shared mesh, where the vertex count is roughly the
// triangle count rather than three times it).
//
// What it costs is the all-three-vertices gate: a triangle straddling the
// visibility boundary is no longer refused whole. The renderer resolves albedo
// per triangle from its provoking vertex, so such a triangle takes one class
// for its whole face, and the textured region grows by up to one triangle at an
// occlusion silhouette. The uv encoding below is what keeps that bounded.
//
// THREE OUTCOMES, not two, and the middle one is the new part:
//   * visible                  -> uv in [0,1]: sample the atlas here.
//   * in frame but occluded    -> -uv - 1: use the vertex colour, but CARRY the
//                                 uv. Still negative, so gfx's `uv0.x < 0` test
//                                 is unchanged; gfx decodes it back rather than
//                                 substituting (0,0). Without this a mixed
//                                 triangle interpolates from a real uv toward
//                                 the atlas origin and smears the corner of the
//                                 image across its face -- which is exactly
//                                 what a shared mesh used to render along every
//                                 silhouette.
//   * not in frame at all      -> (-1,-1), the original sentinel. There is no
//                                 uv to carry, and it decodes to (0,0), which
//                                 is what gfx already substituted.
//
// Every vertex is written on every call, so a vertex that leaves the view
// reverts rather than keeping a stale coordinate -- the property the per-
// triangle version had and this must not lose.
//
// Ported from the prior engine's scoreTrianglesKernel + writeTriangleUVsKernel
// (projective_mapping.cu / projective_texturing.metal): the per-vertex
// bounds + bilinear-depth occlusion test, and camera_uv_to_atlas_uv's +0.5
// half-texel and half-texel clamp. The multi-camera score + winner-take-all
// atomic stay dropped: one camera means no ranking. They come back for a packed
// multi-keyframe atlas, which needs a per-PRIMITIVE camera id and is not what
// vertex sharing needed -- a triangle whose vertices index different sub-rects
// of a pack cannot be expressed per vertex at all, encoded or not.

#extension GL_GOOGLE_include_directive : require
#include "texture_common.glsl"

layout(local_size_x = 256) in;

// No Indices binding: this dispatch never addresses a vertex through an index,
// which is also why it needs no num_vertices bound on that addressing (the
// malformed-mesh guard the per-triangle version carried) -- the only index it
// forms is its own invocation id.
layout(set = 0, binding = 0, scalar) buffer Vertices { Vertex vertices[]; };
layout(set = 0, binding = 1, scalar) readonly buffer Depth { float depth[]; };
layout(set = 0, binding = 2, scalar) readonly buffer Camera {
  DepthCameraParams cam;
};

// Bilinear depth sample at pixel (u, v), in metres, excluding invalid (<= 0)
// taps from the blend and renormalizing by the surviving weight -- so a hole
// (sensor no-return, 0) next to a valid reading does not pull the result toward
// 0 and cause a false occlusion. When the valid taps straddle a depth
// discontinuity (their range exceeds occlusion_threshold), the 2x2 spans a
// foreground/background surface edge and blending would yield a phantom
// mid-depth that fails the occlusion test on a genuinely visible foreground
// vertex; fall back to the nearest (max-weight) valid tap instead, the same
// intent as tsdf_integrate.comp's discontinuity guard (keyed here to the
// occlusion tolerance -- the scale at which the blend must be trustworthy --
// since this tier carries no trunc_dist). Sampling is integer-centred, matching
// this tier's integer-centred projection (u = fx*x/z + cx) and half-texel atlas
// UV; it deliberately does NOT copy the tsdf sampler's -0.5 texture-centred tap
// shift, which is self-consistent only with that tier's texture-centred
// convention (DECISIONS.md, the 2026-07-06 depth-sampling decision). Ported in
// spirit from the prior engine's sample_depth_bilinear_m (our depth is already
// float metres, so there is no uint16 depth-scale divide). Returns 0.0 when all
// four taps are invalid; occluded_ok's range check then rejects it.
float sample_depth(float u, float v) {
  int w = int(cam.width);
  int h = int(cam.height);
  float cu = clamp(u, 0.0, float(w - 1));
  float cv = clamp(v, 0.0, float(h - 1));
  int x0 = int(floor(cu));
  int y0 = int(floor(cv));
  int x1 = min(x0 + 1, w - 1);
  int y1 = min(y0 + 1, h - 1);
  float tx = cu - float(x0);
  float ty = cv - float(y0);

  float d00 = depth[y0 * w + x0];
  float d10 = depth[y0 * w + x1];
  float d01 = depth[y1 * w + x0];
  float d11 = depth[y1 * w + x1];
  float wts[4] = float[4]((1.0 - tx) * (1.0 - ty), tx * (1.0 - ty),
                          (1.0 - tx) * ty, tx * ty);
  float sm[4] = float[4](d00, d10, d01, d11);

  // One pass: blend the valid (> 0) taps, and track their range [lo, hi] and the
  // nearest (max-weight) valid tap for the discontinuity fallback.
  float total = 0.0;
  float result = 0.0;
  float lo = 3.4e38;  // ~FLT_MAX; first valid tap lowers it
  float hi = 0.0;     // depths are positive; first valid tap raises it
  float d_near = 0.0;
  float best_w = -1.0;
  for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
    if (sm[i] > 0.0) {  // a comparison with NaN is false, so NaN taps drop too
      result += wts[i] * sm[i];
      total += wts[i];
      lo = min(lo, sm[i]);
      hi = max(hi, sm[i]);
      if (wts[i] > best_w) {
        best_w = wts[i];
        d_near = sm[i];
      }
    }
  }
  if (total <= 0.0) {
    return 0.0;  // no valid tap; occluded_ok's range check rejects this
  }
  if ((hi - lo) > pc.occlusion_threshold) {
    return d_near;  // depth discontinuity: nearest tap, do not blend fg + bg
  }
  return result / total;
}

// True when the sensor surface at `px` sits within occlusion_threshold of the
// vertex's camera-space depth `zc` -- i.e. the vertex is on the visible surface,
// not hidden behind nearer geometry. Uses a 1-px inset so the bilinear taps
// never straddle the image border (the prior engine's occlusion-bounds margin),
// and rejects a hole / NaN / inf / out-of-range depth via the sensor range (the
// negated compare drops non-finite depth).
bool occluded_ok(vec2 px, float zc) {
  // Negated rather than `px.x < 1.0 || ...`, which is the same work and rejects
  // a NaN instead of admitting one: every comparison with NaN is false, so the
  // direct form falls THROUGH to sample_depth, whose clamp and floor are
  // undefined on a NaN and whose depth[] index has nothing further to bound it.
  // project_to_image refuses a non-finite pixel before this is reached, so the
  // form is what keeps the predicate true on its own terms rather than only on
  // its caller's.
  if (!(px.x >= 1.0 && px.x < float(int(cam.width) - 1) && px.y >= 1.0 &&
        px.y < float(int(cam.height) - 1))) {
    return false;
  }
  float d = sample_depth(px.x, px.y);
  if (!(d >= cam.min_depth && d <= cam.max_depth)) {
    return false;  // hole / non-finite / out-of-range: no line of sight proof
  }
  return abs(d - zc) <= pc.occlusion_threshold;
}

// pixel coords -> normalized atlas UV. The atlas is the camera image itself
// (single-camera slice: atlas_x_offset 0, atlas dims == image dims), so the
// prior engine's camera_uv_to_atlas_uv reduces to a +0.5 half-texel (pixel
// centre -> texel) normalized by the image size, clamped half a texel inside
// the border to stop bilinear bleed at the atlas edge. No y-flip.
vec2 pixel_to_atlas_uv(vec2 px) {
  float w = float(cam.width);
  float h = float(cam.height);
  float u = (px.x + 0.5) / w;
  float v = (px.y + 0.5) / h;
  float u_min = 0.5 / w;
  float v_min = 0.5 / h;
  u = clamp(u, u_min, 1.0 - u_min);
  v = clamp(v, v_min, 1.0 - v_min);
  return vec2(u, v);
}

void main() {
  uint vid = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;
  if (vid >= pc.num_vertices) {
    return;
  }

  // The domain widened with the dispatch: the per-triangle version visited only
  // vertices an index named, and this visits every slot in [0, num_vertices).
  // On a mesh from the sharing kernel those are not the same set -- that kernel
  // publishes a vertex count summed from each block's RESERVATION, while a
  // block that claims fewer than it reserved leaves the tail of its range
  // never written, inside the published count and in nobody's index buffer.
  // Nothing zeroes the arena, so such a slot holds whatever the last extract to
  // own those bytes left. Reading it is harmless -- it is our own buffer, in
  // range -- but the bytes are arbitrary, so the position may be non-finite.
  // That is why project_to_image and occluded_ok are both written to reject a
  // NaN rather than to accept one by default: an unclaimed slot takes the
  // sentinel path and is never referenced by a triangle anyway.
  vec2 px;
  float zc;
  if (!project_to_image(cam, vertices[vid].position, px, zc)) {
    // No usable projection -- behind the camera, where the pinhole divide has
    // no meaning, or a position that is not a number. Either way there
    // genuinely is no coordinate to carry and the bare sentinel is the honest
    // value. The renderer reads it as (0, 0), which is arbitrary -- but a
    // triangle with a vertex behind the camera and another in front spans the
    // near plane, and no per-vertex uv can describe that however it is
    // encoded. Written rather than left, so a vertex that has left the view
    // drops its previous frame's coordinate.
    vertices[vid].uv0 = vec2(-1.0, -1.0);
    return;
  }

  // In FRONT -- not necessarily in frame. The projection is defined either way,
  // so compute the coordinate unconditionally and let the tests below decide
  // only how it is marked. `pixel_to_atlas_uv` clamps an extrapolated pixel
  // half a texel inside the border, so a vertex outside the image carries the
  // nearest edge coordinate rather than a wild one.
  //
  // Carrying it is the whole point at the frustum boundary. Such a vertex is
  // never textured -- `occluded_ok` refuses a pixel within a texel of the edge
  // -- but a triangle whose provoking vertex IS textured interpolates across
  // it, and the bare sentinel would drag that interpolation to the atlas
  // origin, drawing the entire camera image inside one triangle along the whole
  // boundary.
  vec2 uv = pixel_to_atlas_uv(px);
  vertices[vid].uv0 = occluded_ok(px, zc) ? uv : (-uv - 1.0);
}
